#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# polynomial_lcm

#% text
# `polynomial_lcm(p, q, ...)` gives the least common multiple of the polynomials.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is an expanded polynomial, normalized to a positive leading coefficient.

#% item
# The options of `polynomial_gcd` apply: `modulus: p` gives the monic lcm modulo the prime p; `extension: automatic` (or given algebraic numbers) gives the lcm over the number field, `p (q / gcd(p, q))`; `trig: true` as for `polynomial_gcd`. Other options are an argument failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
polynomial_lcm(x^2 - 1, x^2 - 2*x + 1)
#% output
#= 1 - x - x^2 + x^3

#% cell
polynomial_lcm(x, x^2 + x)
#% output
#= x + x^2

#% cell
polynomial_lcm(x - 1, x + 1, x^2 + 1)
#% output
#= -1 + x^4

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The common denominator of a sum of fractions:

#% cell
polynomial_lcm(x^2 - 1, x^2 + x)
#% output
#= -x + x^3

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The result is divisible by each argument:

#% cell
m = polynomial_lcm(x^2 - 1, x^3 - x^2); [polynomial_remainder(m, x^2 - 1, x), polynomial_remainder(m, x^3 - x^2, x)]
#% output
#= [0, 0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `polynomial_gcd`, `lcm`, `together`
